  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10501v1</id>
    <title>Sampling Is Not Curiosity: Why LLM Agents Should Investigate</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T06:02:37Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10501v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10501v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>LLM agents are increasingly used for open-ended tasks, very common in research scenarios where they are required to explore, investigate, or act curiously. At inference time, an LLM is a fixed map from context to next-token distribution, the pretrained weights determine the function, and only its conditioning changes as context accumulates. Mechanisms like temperature, best-of-N, tree search, or RAG vary how the distribution is sampled or what it is conditioned on, but do not update the agent's model of the environment. We argue that current LLM agents do not explore curiously; they explore stochastically. From how curiosity is understood in the RL literature, it requires a predictive structure over how the environment responds to the agent's actions, which improves online from current interaction, drives action selection through its predicted improvement, and persists beyond the context window. Additionally, in its open-ended form, the agent should intrinsically originate what to investigate. Thus, we propose a framework defining curiosity in LLM agents, reserving the term for systems that satisfy it. In doing so, we identify the research directions its implementation opens up.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T06:02:37Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to the 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Position Paper Track</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Alfonso Amayuelas</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Piotr Piękos</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xin Eric Wang</name>
    </author>
