  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10510v1</id>
    <title>ReSkill: Selective Self-Improvement for Robot Manipulation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T06:21:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10510v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10510v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Self-improvement on compositional manipulation tasks requires deciding what to reuse, where to improve, and which changes to retain. We present ReSkill, a selective self-improvement framework that concentrates revision on capability gaps. A coding agent constructs an object-centric task graph from an instruction and a skill library, then uses node-level execution records to locate failures. At the task level, it revises stage organization, hand-off conditions, and recovery routes; at the skill level, it strengthens perception, motion, manipulation, and verification capabilities that repeatedly fail. Candidate revisions undergo full-task evaluation and paired comparisons before adoption, allowing local changes to accumulate into validated skills and an independently executable policy. The agent participates only during development; the deployed policy calls the retained skills without invoking the agent at run time. On the same development set, average success across ten RoboDojo tasks rises from 30.0% for the initial versions to 86.2% for the final adopted programs; retained programs achieve 83.6% in recorded robustness and model-selection evaluations. Across eight real-world tasks, ReSkill succeeds in 72 of 80 rebuilt-simulation trials and 62 of 80 real-robot trials (77.5%). The median revision changes only three functions, approximately 0.5% of a program, while 39% of task-specific functions in later-added tasks directly reuse existing implementations. These results show how locating local deficiencies, validating candidate changes, and accumulating reusable skills can improve execution of compositional manipulation tasks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T06:21:09Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Submitted to intelligent computing for embodied AI and robotics</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Zheng Sun</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhihao Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhuo Li</name>
    </author>
