  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12120v1</id>
    <title>SCALE: Small-Model-First Concept Abstraction with LLM-Guided Experience Internalization</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T09:33:19Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12120v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12120v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Autonomous agents must continually acquire reusable knowledge from experience without relying on costly large language model (LLM) reasoning for every decision. However, existing experience-based approaches often struggle to transform accumulated observations and reasoning into compact knowledge that lightweight models can independently apply. This paper proposes SCALE, a small-model-first framework that integrates selective LLM reasoning, experience-driven concept abstraction, and knowledge internalization. A lightweight model handles routine predictions, while prediction errors and accumulated cross-episode evidence selectively trigger LLM reflection. The LLM analyzes relevant historical experiences, generates positive and negative counterfactual examples, and proposes reusable concept hypotheses. Independently validated concepts are progressively incorporated into hierarchical neural representations, enabling lightweight models to exploit increasingly abstract knowledge. Meanwhile, mastered experiences are excluded from active memory while their original records remain available in a complete archive. Experiments across ten random seeds show that historical recall improves held-out accuracy from 87.11% to 98.28%, hierarchical concept knowledge increases difficult-case accuracy from 55.33% to 66.47%, and abstract archiving reduces active experience records from 6,000 to 1,528.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T09:33:19Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hong Su</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12885v1</id>
    <title>World In-Context Learning for Agentic Robot Manipulation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:59:45Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12885v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12885v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Trained action policies write manipulation skill into network weights, tying what is learned to the distribution it is learned from. In-context learning (ICL) avoids this coupling but has not matched trained policies, because a demonstration recorded in another scene carries no feedback from the world the robot currently faces. We observe that what an agent learns in context is semantic, recording task steps and object roles rather than visual appearance, so experience gathered in one world transfers to another. Based on this insight, we propose World In-Context Learning (WICL), a paradigm in which a frozen model starts from a demonstration, rehearses the task in a world such as a simulator, and writes the feedback into its context rather than its weights. We build RoboScript, a modular infrastructure that implements WICL for agentic manipulation. From a single demonstration, RoboScript learns each task through rehearsal in simulation, freezes the learned knowledge as a Task Memory, and deploys the frozen memory on new scenes with data isolation that ensures every deployment input is available on a real robot. On all 50 tasks of RoboTwin 2.0, RoboScript reaches 83.4% mean success across clean and randomized settings with one demonstration per task and no gradient updates, exceeding the strongest trained action policy by over 20 points. The method generalizes across visual distributions, improves through reflection, scales with the foundation model, and transfers to a physical dual-arm robot through cross-embodiment memory adaptation. With a world in the loop, a robot can learn a manipulation task in its context as well as it learns one in its weights. All code is available at https://github.com/ArcheOne-AI/RoboScript</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:59:45Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project Page: https://ArcheOne-AI.github.io/RoboScript</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Heng Fang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zehui Guo</name>
    </author>
