  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11565v1</id>
    <title>Actions Should EARN Their Credit: Test-Time Adaptation and Route Consolidation for Frozen LLM Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-08T19:22:35Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11565v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11565v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>LLM agents can use feedback from repeated attempts to improve their decisions without updating model parameters. However, positive action credits can continue to favor actions that repeatedly achieve the same score, even when higher scores are achievable. Under a fixed interaction budget, trying new actions in the final attempts can also lower performance. We introduce EARN, a method that uses past experience to guide further progress and retain earlier performance through conditional reuse of effective action sequences. EARN reduces action credits for reaching scores repeatedly attained in earlier episodes and assigns larger credits to action segments that reach higher scores. These credits adjust the probabilities of LLM-proposed actions. During the final episodes, EARN directly replays stored actions when the current context matches the recorded context and the actions remain available, bypassing LLM candidate generation. We evaluate performance at the end of the interaction budget using the average score over the final four episodes. Across Jericho, ScienceWorld, and Crafter, EARN achieves the best score on this metric in 31 of 33 task--budget settings among the evaluated methods, including ties. Across the evaluated Jericho budgets, replay bypasses 78.9--86.3\% of candidate-generation calls in the final four episodes, averaged across games.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T19:22:35Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Bian Sun</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kevin Zhai</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mubarak Shah</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Song Wang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11509v1</id>
    <title>Review the Episodes, Refine the Lessons: Simple Memory Evolution for Embodied Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-08T17:33:29Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11509v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11509v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recent studies of embodied agents based on frontier multimodal large language models (MLLMs) develop harness engineering around the MLLM, such as harnesses with post-trained policies and recursive self-improvement loops. Yet whether these additions exceed the native capability of the MLLM remains unexplored. We introduce SimplEvo to explore how far an MLLM can improve under direct control when it retains its execution experience in self-written text memories. It requires no weight updates, post-trained policies, or privileged information. The MLLM writes a coordinate-free task memory once from a single demonstration. Under a strict train-test protocol, it then creates a bounded working memory on training episodes only and refines it after each episode with lessons on procedure, completion, and recovery. Both memories stay fixed at test time. On EBench, SimplEvo lifts the success rate (SR) of GPT-6-Astra under direct control from 46.7% to 81.7%, and tops the RoboDojo leaderboard with 69.63% SR and a score of 75.32. Compared with memories that also store past visual frames, SimplEvo reaches a similar SR with text memory alone while using fewer tokens. With its memories fixed, SimplEvo also generalizes to unseen layouts and randomized scene configurations. Moreover, the working memory transfers across models: written by GPT-6-Astra, it can be reused as is by weaker MLLMs and improve their performance without any adaptation of their own.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T17:33:29Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yifei Yao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yude Zou</name>
    </author>
