  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10787v1</id>
    <title>Trustworthy Recursive Self-Improvement with Statistical Guarantees</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T15:12:01Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10787v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10787v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recursive self-improvement offers a promising path toward AI agents that improve through experience by refining their own harnesses without retraining their underlying large language models (LLMs). Making this self-improvement process reliable, however, requires more than generating new updates: agents must decide which changes are worth keeping. A greedy gate that accepts updates based solely on higher validation scores can mistake an observed gain for genuine progress and lead to performance degeneration, whereas overly conservative statistical gates may reject beneficial updates and hinder performance improvement. We introduce Trustworthy Recursive Updates with Statistical Testing (TRUST), a framework that adapts an online multiple hypothesis testing procedure to control the rate of false evolution across rounds, with an LLM analyzing historical context to help determine how much evidence is required to update the incumbent. Under interpretable assumptions, we prove that TRUST limits the proportion of accepted updates that result in unacceptable performance loss. We further derive a lower bound on the final performance gain. Across the benchmarks HotpotQA, DROP, and APPS with the models DeepSeek-V4-Flash and Qwen-3.8-Flash, TRUST reduces the average ratio of accepted inferior updates from the greedy gate's 45.59% to 2.78%, while achieving higher final scores than the state-of-the-art statistical gate baselines for recursive self-improvement. These findings demonstrate that TRUST strikes a balance between statistical reliability and performance gain in recursive self-improvement.</summary>
    <category term="stat.ML" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="stat.ME" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T15:12:01Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>44 pages, including appendix</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="stat.ML"/>
    <author>
      <name>Zihe Gao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhengling Qi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yifan Cui</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10542v1</id>
    <title>When Experience Becomes a Backdoor: Poisoning Self-Evolving LLM Agents via Failure-Aware Task Optimization</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T07:17:53Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10542v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10542v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly improve themselves through self-evolution, distilling execution experience into persistent, reusable skills. However, this persistence creates a new attack surface: malicious rules implanted into skills can compromise subsequent agent behavior. Existing skill backdoor attacks typically assume direct manipulation of the victim's skills, which is impractical when skills are constructed internally by a trusted evolver. In this paper, we study a novel problem of task-level backdoor attacks, where the attacker can only inject poisoned evolution tasks, inducing the agent to implant backdoors into its own skills. Since the attacker controls neither skill induction nor downstream execution, a natural approach is to iteratively optimize poisoned tasks based on attack success. However, our preliminary analysis reveals two key challenges: (i) failures at different stages of skill evolution and downstream execution can produce indistinguishable outcomes, providing little guidance for targeted poisoned-task updates; and (ii) unsuccessful updates may undermine earlier progress, while simply discarding them may lead to repeated ineffective updates. To address these challenges, we propose BadEvolve, a failure-aware task-poisoning framework against self-evolving LLM agents. BadEvolve diagnoses attack failures across four stages, i.e., Induction, Activation, Following, and Effect, and directs updates toward the failing stage. It further employs rollback to preserve earlier progress and failure memory to avoid repeating ineffective updates. Extensive experiments across diverse self-evolution frameworks, benchmarks, and backbone LLMs demonstrate the effectiveness of BadEvolve. Our code is available at https://github.com/ventr1c/BadEvolve.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T07:17:53Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Minhua Lin</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zongyu Wu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hanqing Lu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xianfeng Tang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiang Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qi He</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Suhang Wang</name>
    </author>
