  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10477v1</id>
    <title>PatternBloom: Empowering Agentic RAG with Externalized RL-Distilled Graph Patterns</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T05:02:26Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10477v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10477v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reinforcement-learning-trained agents for retrieval-augmented generation retrieve content (passages, knowledge-graph fragments, or hyperedges) and supervise training with outcome rewards on the final answer. Across these paradigms the reasoning route through retrieved content never leaves the language model's hidden state, conflating declarative knowledge with the procedural knowledge that recurs across queries of the same logical type; content-only methods consequently plateau as reasoning depth grows. We introduce PatternBloom, an agentic RAG framework that shifts the unit of retrieval from content (what to read) to logic (how to reason). A first RL stage scores the agent's constructed evidence graph by a frozen oracle's information gain through the Information-Density Reward (IDR), a size-normalized mutual-information surrogate. High-reward trajectories are distilled into a Graph Pattern Memory (GPM) of type-abstracted reasoning skeletons, and a second RL stage uses the Pattern-Augmented Reward (PAR) to couple policy to memory, turning procedural reasoning into a learnable, externalized structural prior. A 7B PatternBloom outperforms $27$ baselines by $+15.0$ Avg-OOD EM, with the gain compounding with reasoning depth precisely because procedural knowledge, once distilled, is reused rather than re-derived at every query. We release our code at https://github.com/EllenYiGe/PatternBloom.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T05:02:26Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted at NeurIPS 2026. 46 pages. Code: https://github.com/EllenYiGe/PatternBloom</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yi Ge</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Heng Huang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10022v1</id>
    <title>How Much Skill Orchestration Can LLM Agents Compile Away? Execution-Grounded Specialization for Token-Efficient Skill Use</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T00:01:07Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10022v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10022v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reusable skills guide large language model (LLM) agents through recurring requests, but each invocation still requires the model to interpret the skill and reconstruct its orchestration. Can agents automatically compile this orchestration into an executable fast path without human intervention? We first study zero-shot skill specialization, which compiles without execution evidence, and find that despite the skill's procedural guidance, incorrect assumptions about runtime bindings can make generated plans brittle and recovery costly. This motivates SkillCache, a one-trace skill specialization method that compiles a completed invocation into a parameterized capsule of programs and natural-language steps, replays it before reuse, and drives it with a plan executor that returns control to the model only when needed, keeping the source skill for recovery. With the Claude Code harness, warm execution of the same request reduces tokens on 87 SkillsBench tasks by 81% with Claude Sonnet 4.6 and by 74% with Claude Opus 4.8 at comparable reward, and capsules stay effective under controlled request changes. Capsules also transfer across models, raising three open-weight models' mean reward by 12-25 percentage points while reducing their tokens by 72-81%, suggesting that execution-grounded compilation is a promising approach to efficient repeated skill use and cross-model transfer.</summary>
    <category term="cs.SE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T00:01:07Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>35 pages, 20 figures, 16 tables. An earlier version has been selected for an oral/spotlight presentation at the AgenticOS workshop at NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.SE"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jiayi Ni</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zac Wang</name>
    </author>
